+\runin{Standard reservoirs operate far below the limit.}The simulations do not test the bound; they locate standard reservoirs relative to it. We simulated transverse-field Ising reservoirs with random all-to-all couplings, the workhorse of QRC~\cite{Fujii2017,Nakajima2019,MartinezPena2021}, and Haar-random unitaries at $q=0.9$, and estimated their memory depth $W$ at $\varepsilon=0.01$~\cite{SM}. Within these ensembles, size does not extend the memory [Fig.~\ref{fig:attain}(c)]: with one input qubit, the Ising reservoir remembers 16 to 18 time steps for every size from $n=3$ to $9$, and the Haar reservoir forgets faster as it grows ($W$ drops from 9 to 4). More input qubits raise the ceiling but, in generic dynamics, lower the memory, because reinitialization also erases what those qubits had stored: at $n=8$, raising $k$ from 1 to 4 lowers $W$ from 18 to 8 time steps (Ising) and from 4 to 3 (Haar), even when the additional qubits are reinitialized in $\ket0$~\cite{SM}. Both stay below an idle qubit that merely holds the input ($W=43$), whereas the error-correcting reservoir of the same size ($n=8$, $k=5$) remembers over 300 time steps. The difference lies in how the dynamics uses the fresh qubits.
 
 \runin{Beyond reservoirs: an entropy budget.}The same bound covers other noise and refresh operations: with a fixed local depolarizing component, additional channels with total budget $b$ replace $k$ by $k+b$~\cite[Sec.~VI]{SM}. Pauli errors, dephasing, coherent errors, coherent crosstalk, and projective measurements whose outcomes only enter the readout~\cite{Mujal2023} are unital: their zero budget does not raise the upper bound. Every unital qubit channel with full-rank Choi matrix contains a depolarizing component, so the theorem covers generic independent unital noise~\cite[Sec.~VI]{SM}. Entropy-removing channels contribute a positive budget. Resetting $m$ qubits adds $m$ bits. Amplitude damping with probability $0<\gamma<1$ adds $n\log_2(1+\gamma)$ bits; relaxing idle qubits then act as a refrigerator~\cite{BenOr2013}, allowing a reservoir with two input qubits to remember for a time exponential in $n$~\citetext{\citealp[Sec.~VI]{SM}; \citealp{Hoeffding1963}}. With no additional entropy sink, a measurement-free recovery using $k$ fresh ancillas per round belongs, together with its data qubits, to $\mathcal N$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:scope}]. After initialization washout, it can protect a logical bit for at most $\exp[O_q(k)]$ rounds, regardless of size. Classical syndrome feedback is outside the model and can lift the ceiling~\cite[Sec.~VI]{SM}. Without a depolarizing component or with non-Markovian noise~\cite{Sannia2026}, memory can be unbounded~\cite[Sec.~VI]{SM}.
 
